<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adal Sultanate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Adal, Adalite</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Adal Sultanate was a Muslim state in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>, centred on the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a>, which flourished from the fifteenth to the sixteenth century. It controlled trade routes linking the Ethiopian interior to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-aden">Gulf of Aden</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi">Imam Ahmad</a> ibn Ibrahim al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a> it invaded and devastated much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>, before declining after his death in 1543.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate.jpg" alt="Adal Sultanate" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FDRMRZUSA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Adal_Sultanate.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
